## Handling Database Transactions

When testing database-related features with Laravel Dusk, it's often useful to roll back any changes made during the test to ensure a clean database state for subsequent tests. You can use the `DatabaseTransactions` trait in your test to accomplish this.

```php
namespace Tests\Browser;

use Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\DatabaseTransactions;

class ExampleTest extends TestCase
{
    use DatabaseTransactions;

    // ...
}
```

You can also apply the trait on a per-test basis by adding `use DatabaseTransactions;` to the test method itself.

```php
public function testDatabaseExample()
{
    use DatabaseTransactions;

    // Your test code goes here.
}
```

## Testing AJAX Requests

Laravel Dusk provides simple methods to handle AJAX requests. Here's an example of how to test a simple AJAX request:

```php
namespace Tests\Browser;

class ExampleTest extends TestCase
{
    public function testAjax()
    {
        $this->browse(function (Browser $browser) {
            $browser->visit('/ajax')
                ->clickLink('Make AJAX request')
                ->assertSee('AJAX request completed successfully');
        });
    }
}
```

This example assumes your application has a route that responds to a simple AJAX request at `/ajax`. In this case, the route is assumed to render a view with the text "AJAX request completed successfully" when the AJAX request is successful.

For more complex AJAX requests, you can use Dusk's `driver()->driver()->execute()` method to interact with the browser's JavaScript environment directly. Here's an example of how to test a more complex AJAX request that submits a form using JavaScript:

```php
